who could influence Sadleir.  I don't understand the "this is not
censorship" argument, for the groups that were eliminated from UTCC
strongly indicate a political motive.  The administration was basically
acting out of fear of reprisal.  I saw a quote on the front page of the
Globe talking about "obeying the laws of the country", not that anyone
can be sure that they were breaking the law.  The Universities are more or 
less being scared into censoring themselves.

>Have you ever tried to explain networking, news, email,
>mail-based-archive-servers or the like to a reporter?  I have.  I have
>had better luck explaining it to toddlers.  I will no longer talk to
>reporters as a result.  They've never failed to distort the meaning of
>my words.
I am amused by the way they refer to them as "electronic bulletin boards".
They do tend to emphasize the negative side of the internet.  Perhaps
they feel threatened by it.

>Central administration (at the highest level) would have little
>choice but to respond the way they did to a story as presented.  This
>topic is a very political topic.  Don't blame Warren Jackson.  He's not
>the one who made the decree to ban newsgroups, and he did not tell
>anyone  at ECF to delete or suspend your computer access.

I asked, why not just a simple e-mail warning that I was in contradiction
with the rules.  This is apparently their standard warning (which I took
to be much more than that at the time).  My subversive activity had to be
stopped until I could make my way to their office to explain myself. I
guess I can't complain really, just be aware that sys admins tend to be
very cautious about the information that is posted from their machines.

